\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Elements of this chapter. \code {gauss\_order} is the attribute of the class as it stands in release v1.0.1\,/\,v0.2.0; its meaning is the number of points per direction for quadrilaterals and hexahedra, and a simplex rule order for triangles and tetrahedra.\relax }}{7}{table.caption.7}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Tip deflection of a slender cantilever divided by the Euler--Bernoulli value, three hexahedra and two Poisson ratios. The 20-node mesh has the node spacing of the 8-node mesh. A 3-D beam does not reach exactly 1 even for a perfect element (root and shear effects), so only the differences between the rows are significant.\relax }}{8}{table.caption.12}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Tip deflection of a flat cantilever strip divided by the beam-theory value with plate rigidity (an approximate reference), against the number of elements along the length (a quarter as many across).\relax }}{8}{table.caption.14}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Patch tests on distorted meshes. The test helper is \texttt {\_patch} of \texttt {test\_benchmarks\_convergence.py}.\relax }}{9}{table.caption.16}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Where the concepts of this chapter live in the repository (release v1.0.1\,/\,v0.2.0).\relax }}{10}{table.caption.19}%
